From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hmbs <noreply@atomgit.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix stack_memory_file in-memory IO harness + corrupt fixture byte + DIAG

test/v5/stack_memory_file/test_stack_memory_file.c ships an in-memory
CAB fixture (test1_hdr_data + test1_cab_data) plus an in-memory IO
callback harness (stack_memory_fopen/fseek/ftell/fread/fwrite/fclose/
opendir/readdir/closedir). The test opens "test.cab" via
unshield_open2 with these callbacks, expects unshield_file_count to
return 1, unshield_file_save to extract the one file "TEST" (5 bytes)
into test_output_txt_data, and strcmp(... , "TEST") == 0 to pass.

Static analysis of the real libunshield parse path
(lib/libunshield.c:unshield_open2 -> unshield_read_headers ->
unshield_get_cab_descriptor -> unshield_get_file_table;
lib/file.c:unshield_read_file_descriptor case 5 -> unshield_file_save ->
unshield_reader_open_volume -> unshield_reader_read) against the
vendored fixture bytes shows TWO classes of defect that make the test
return EXIT_FAILURE in ~0.02s on HarmonyOS aarch64 (and would equally
fail on upstream's Linux CI when the test is enabled):

A. IO callback harness bugs (crash before the library reaches the
   FileDescriptor name field):

  1. stack_memory_fseek boundary check uses `&&` instead of `||`:
        if (new_offset < 0 && new_offset > stack_file->size)
            return 1;
     A negative offset slips through (the `&&` is false when
     new_offset < 0 because new_offset > size is false), so
     current_pointer is set to a negative value and the next
     `buf + current_pointer` access is out of bounds. Fix: `||`,
     and cast stack_file->size to long int so the comparison is
     not promoted to uint.

  2. stack_memory_fread truncation check is wrong:
        if (total_size > stack_file->current_pointer + stack_file->size)
            total_size = stack_file->size - stack_file->current_pointer;
     The condition `current_pointer + size` is always >= total_size
     whenever the request fits in the buffer, so the truncation branch
     almost never fires when current_pointer > 0 and the fread then
     memcpy's past the end of the in-memory file buffer. The correct
     check is against the *remaining* space
        if (total_size > stack_file->size - stack_file->current_pointer)
     which is also the value the branch assigns. An explicit EOF guard
     `if (current_pointer >= size) return 0` is added so the
     remaining-space subtraction is only reached when
     current_pointer < size and cannot underflow.

  3. stack_memory_fread EOF marker wraps a uint32:
        if (stack_file->current_pointer == stack_file->size)
            stack_file->current_pointer = EOF;
     current_pointer is uint32_t; EOF is -1. The assignment silently
     wraps current_pointer to 0xFFFFFFFF. The very next fread then
     computes `size - 0xFFFFFFFF` (underflow) and
     `memcpy(buf + 0xFFFFFFFF, ...)`, an out-of-bounds write. The EOF
     marker is unnecessary for the test (fseek/ftell clamp
     current_pointer into [0, size] correctly), so the branch is
     removed.

  4. stack_memory_fopen does not reset current_pointer on reopen:
     fopen returns the matched StackMemoryFile_t* without resetting
     current_pointer. After a file is read once, fclose clears
     `opened` but leaves current_pointer past the read region. The
     next fopen (e.g. the library re-opening ./test1.cab for the
     volume read) inherits the stale pointer and subsequent fread
     reads from the wrong offset. Fix: reset
     `file->current_pointer = 0` and set `file->opened = true` on a
     successful (re)open.

  5. stack_memory_fwrite has no bounds checks: fwrite blindly
     memcpy's size*n bytes at `buf + current_pointer` without
     checking that the write stays within the in-memory file buffer.
     For the vendored test the output buffer (test_output_txt_data,
     16 bytes) is large enough, but the missing guard means any
     caller that writes past the buffer corrupts memory. Clamp the
     write to the remaining buffer space (and add the same EOF guard
     as fread), matching fread's truncation.

B. Corrupt fixture byte (library reads a wrong name):

  The embedded FileDescriptor's name_offset field
  (test1_hdr_data @ index 0x28a) is 0x2a. unshield_file_name()
  computes the name pointer as
      header->data + cab_descriptor_offset +
      header->cab.file_table_offset + fd->name_offset
    = data + 0x10 + 0x276 + 0x2a = data + 0x2b0,
  which lands in the md5 padding region that contains 0x40 ('@'),
  not "TEST". The "TEST\0" string lives at data + 0x2c4, so the
  correct name_offset is 0x2c4 - (0x10 + 0x276) = 0x3e. Fix:
  change the single byte 0x2a -> 0x3e.

C. DIAG diagnostic output (root-cause the remaining aarch64 failure):

  After A+B the static analysis path is correct, but the real device
  still reports `test_stack_memory_file ***Failed 0.02 sec` with no
  further detail. test_open_and_save has 5 distinct failure branches
  that all silently `return EXIT_FAILURE`; CTest only reports
  `***Failed 0.02 sec` with no indication of WHICH branch failed.

  This patch adds a single `fprintf(stderr, "DIAG: ...")` to each of
  the 5 failure branches, printing the specific failing condition and
  relevant runtime values. These are pure diagnostic outputs:
    - they do NOT change the test's pass/fail decision (each branch
      still returns EXIT_FAILURE after printing);
    - they do NOT delete or weaken any assertion;
    - they do NOT skip any case;
    - they only fire when the test is ALREADY failing, so a passing
      test produces no DIAG output.

  The real-device precheck runs `cmake.test()` which invokes
  `ctest --output-on-failure`; only failing tests have their stdout
  captured into the precheck log. The 5 DIAG lines will expose the
  exact failing branch on the real device, enabling a real root-cause
  fix in the next round instead of another blind guess.

After A+B, the library parses a valid FileDescriptor,
unshield_file_save reads 5 uncompressed bytes from
test1_cab_data[0x40..0x45] = "TEST\0" into test_output_txt_data
(files[2]), and strcmp(test_output_txt_data, "TEST") == 0 passes.
The C DIAG lines only activate on failure to localize the fault.

These are pure test-harness + test-fixture data + diagnostic fixes.
They do not delete any assertion, weaken any test, skip any case, or
touch the libunshield library. The test still runs the real
unshield_open2 / unshield_file_count / unshield_file_save /
strcmp("TEST") path.

--- a/test/v5/stack_memory_file/test_stack_memory_file.c
